  {
    "title": "Monitoring multiple satellite aerosol optical depth (AOD) products within the Copernicus Atmosphere Monitoring Service (CAMS) data assimilation system",
    "authors": [
      "S. Garrigues",
      "Samuel Remy​​​​​​​",
      "J. Chimot",
      "M. Ades",
      "A. Inness",
      "J. Flemming",
      "Z. Kipling",
      "I. Laszlo",
      "A. Benedetti",
      "Roberto Ribas",
      "S. Jafariserajehlou",
      "B. Fougnie",
      "S. Kondragunta",
      "R. Engelen",
      "V. Peuch",
      "M. Parrington",
      "N. Bousserez",
      "Margarita Vazquez Navarro",
      "A. Agustí-Panareda"
    ],
    "year": 2022,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-22-14657-2022",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 29,
    "paper_id": "b3f8ea0f38c4d5e22cb1435a245d90f2dee44e71",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b3f8ea0f38c4d5e22cb1435a245d90f2dee44e71",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The Copernicus Atmosphere Monitoring Service (CAMS)\nprovides near-real-time forecast and reanalysis of aerosols using the ECMWF\nIntegrated Forecasting System with atmospheric composition extension,\nconstrained by the assimilation of MODIS and the Polar Multi-Sensor Aerosol\nOptical Properties (PMAp) aerosol optical depth (AOD). The objective of this\nwork is to evaluate two new near-real-time AOD products to prepare for their\nassimilation into CAMS, namely the Copernicus AOD (collection 1) from the Sea\nand Land Surface Temperature Radiometer (SLSTR) on board Sentinel 3-A/B\nover ocean and the NOAA EPS AOD (v2.r1) from VIIRS on board S-NPP and\nNOAA20 over both land and ocean. The differences between MODIS (C6.1), PMAp\n(v2.1), VIIRS (v2.r1), and SLSTR (C1) AOD as well as their departure from the\nmodeled AOD were assessed at the model grid resolution (i.e., level-3)\nusing the 3-month AOD average (December 2019–February 2020 and March–May 2020). VIIRS and MODIS show the best consistency across the products, which is\nexplained by instrument and retrieval algorithm similarities. VIIRS AOD is\nfrequently lower over the ocean background and higher over biomass burning\nand dust source land regions compared to MODIS. VIIRS shows larger spatial\ncoverage over land and resolves finer spatial structures such as the\ntransport of Australian biomass burning smoke over the Pacific, which can be\nexplained by the use of a heavy aerosol detection test in the retrieval\nalgorithm. Our results confirm the positive offset over ocean (i) between\nTerra/MODIS and Aqua/MODIS due to the non-corrected radiometric calibration\ndegradation of Terra/MODIS in the Dark Target algorithm and (ii) between\nSNPP/VIIRS and NOAA20/VIIRS due to the positive bias in the solar reflective\nbands of SNPP/VIIRS. SLSTR AOD shows much smaller level-3 values than the\nrest of the products, which is mainly related to differences in spatial\nrepresentativity at the IFS grid spatial resolution due to the stringent\ncloud filtering applied to the SLSTR radiances. Finally, the geometry\ncharacteristics of the instrument, which drive the range of scattering\nangles sampled by the instrument, can explain a large part of the\ndifferences between retrievals such as the positive offset between PMAp datasets from MetOp-B and MetOp-A.\n",
    "indexed": {
