  {
    "title": "Impact of different sources of precursors on an ozone pollution outbreak over Europe analysed with IASI+GOME2 multispectral satellite observations and model simulations",
    "authors": [
      "S. Okamoto",
      "J. Cuesta",
      "M. Beekmann",
      "G. Dufour",
      "M. Eremenko",
      "K. Miyazaki",
      "C. Boonne",
      "H. Tanimoto",
      "H. Akimoto"
    ],
    "year": 2023,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-23-7399-2023",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 7,
    "paper_id": "91dab395b76cdf62f09e8830e40262ae27f22ca7",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/91dab395b76cdf62f09e8830e40262ae27f22ca7",
    "abstract": "Abstract. We examine the impact of different sources of ozone\nprecursors on the daily evolution of successive ozone pollution outbreaks\nacross Europe in July 2017 by using a multispectral satellite approach\ncalled IASI+GOME2 and a tropospheric chemistry reanalysis named TCR-2.\nIASI+GOME2, combining IASI (Infrared Atmospheric Sounding Interferometer)\nand GOME-2 (Global Ozone Monitoring Experiment-2) measurements respectively\nin the infrared and the ultraviolet, allows the observation of the daily\nhorizontal distribution of ozone in the lowermost troposphere (defined here\nas the atmospheric layer between the surface and 3 km above sea level).\nIASI+GOME2 observations show a fair capacity to depict near-surface ozone\nevolution as compared to surface measurements from 188 European stations for the period 15–27 July 2017. At the beginning of this event (on 16 July), an ozone outbreak is initially\nformed over the Iberian Peninsula likely linked with high\ntemperature-induced enhancements of biogenic volatile organic compound\nconcentrations and collocated anthropogenic emissions. In the following\ndays, the ozone plume splits into two branches, one being transported\neastward across the western Mediterranean and Italy and the other one over\nwestern and Central Europe. The southern branch encounters ozone precursors\nemitted over the Balkan Peninsula by wildfires along the coast of the\nAdriatic Sea and biogenic sources in the inland region of the peninsula.\nOzone concentrations of the northern plume are enhanced by photochemical\nproduction associated with anthropogenic sources of ozone precursors over\nCentral Europe and by mixing with an ozone plume arriving from the North Sea\nthat was originally produced over North America. Finally, both ozone\nbranches are transported eastwards and mix gradually as they reach the\nnorthern coast of the Black Sea. There, emissions from agricultural fires\nafter harvesting clearly favour photochemical production of ozone within the\npollution plume, which is advected eastwards in the following days. Based on satellite analysis, this paper shows the interplay of various ozone\nprecursor sources to sustain a 2-week-long ozone pollution event over\ndifferent parts of Europe.\n",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2023,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
