  {
    "title": "Tropospheric NO2 vertical profiles over South Korea and their relation to oxidant chemistry: implications for geostationary satellite retrievals and the observation of NO2 diurnal variation from space",
    "authors": [
      "L. Yang",
      "D. Jacob",
      "N. Colombi",
      "S. Zhai",
      "K. Bates",
      "Viral Shah",
      "E. Beaudry",
      "R. Yantosca",
      "Haipeng Lin",
      "J. F. Brewer",
      "H. Chong",
      "K. Travis",
      "J. Crawford",
      "L. Lamsal",
      "Ja-Ho Koo",
      "Jhoon Kim"
    ],
    "year": 2023,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-23-2465-2023",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 39,
    "paper_id": "d6ca0285e2df2d20d50370f1707d936d44c1040a",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d6ca0285e2df2d20d50370f1707d936d44c1040a",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Nitrogen oxides (NOx≡ NO + NO2) are of central importance for air quality, climate forcing, and nitrogen deposition\nto ecosystems. The Geostationary Environment Monitoring Spectrometer (GEMS)\nis now providing hourly NO2 satellite observations over East Asia,\noffering the first direct measurements of NO2 diurnal variation from\nspace to guide understanding of NOx emissions and chemistry. The\nNO2 retrieval requires independent vertical profile information from a\nchemical transport model (CTM) to compute the air mass factor (AMF) that\nrelates the NO2 column measured along the line of sight to the NO2\nvertical column. Here, we use aircraft observations from the Korea-United\nStates Air Quality (KORUS-AQ) campaign over the Seoul metropolitan area (SMA) and around the Korean Peninsula in May–June 2016 to better understand the factors controlling the NO2 vertical profile, its diurnal\nvariation, the implications for the AMFs, and the ability of the GEOS-Chem CTM to compute the NO2 vertical profiles used for AMFs. Proper representation of oxidant chemistry is critical for the CTM simulation of\nNO2 vertical profiles and is achieved in GEOS-Chem through new model\ndevelopments, including aerosol nitrate photolysis, reduced uptake of hydroperoxy (HO2) radicals by aerosols, and accounting for atmospheric\noxidation of volatile chemical products (VCPs). We find that the\ntropospheric NO2 columns measured from space in the SMA are mainly\ncontributed by the planetary boundary layer (PBL) below 2 km altitude,\nreflecting the highly polluted conditions. Repeated measurements of NO2\nvertical profiles over the SMA at different times of day show that diurnal\nchange in mixing depth affecting the NO2 vertical profile induces a\ndiurnal variation in AMFs of comparable magnitude to the diurnal variation in the NO2 column. GEOS-Chem captures this diurnal variation in AMFs and\nmore generally the variability in the AMFs for the KORUS-AQ NO2 vertical profiles (2.7 % mean bias, 7.6 % precision), with some outliers in the\nmorning due to errors in the timing of mixed-layer growth.\n",
    "indexed": {
