  {
    "title": "Assessing sub-grid variability within satellite pixels over urban regions using airborne mapping spectrometer measurements",
    "authors": [
      "W. Tang",
      "D. Edwards",
      "L. Emmons",
      "H. Worden",
      "L. Judd",
      "L. Lamsal",
      "J. Al-Saadi",
      "S. Janz",
      "J. Crawford",
      "M. Deeter",
      "G. Pfister",
      "R. Buchholz",
      "B. Gaubert",
      "C. Nowlan"
    ],
    "year": 2021,
    "doi": "10.5194/AMT-14-4639-2021",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Measurement Techniques",
    "citation_count": 12,
    "paper_id": "10adef7d309511afb605065476fd266832c21bbb",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/10adef7d309511afb605065476fd266832c21bbb",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Sub-grid variability (SGV) in atmospheric trace gases within satellite\npixels is a key issue in satellite design and interpretation and validation\nof retrieval products. However, characterizing this variability is\nchallenging due to the lack of independent high-resolution measurements.\nHere we use tropospheric NO2 vertical column (VC) measurements from the Geostationary Trace gas and Aerosol Sensor Optimization (GeoTASO) airborne instrument with a spatial resolution of about 250 m×250 m to quantify the normalized SGV (i.e., the standard deviation of the sub-grid GeoTASO values within the sampled satellite pixel divided by the mean of the sub-grid GeoTASO values within the same satellite pixel) for different hypothetical satellite pixel sizes over urban regions. We use the GeoTASO measurements over the Seoul Metropolitan Area (SMA) and Busan region of South Korea during the 2016 KORUS-AQ field campaign and over the Los Angeles Basin, USA, during the 2017 Student\nAirborne Research Program (SARP) field campaign. We find that the normalized SGV of NO2 VC increases with increasing satellite pixel sizes (from ∼10 % for 0.5 km×0.5 km pixel size to ∼35 % for 25 km×25 km pixel size), and this relationship holds for the three study regions, which are also within the domains of upcoming geostationary satellite air quality missions. We also quantify the temporal variability in the retrieved NO2 VC within the same hypothetical satellite pixels (represented by the difference of retrieved values at two or more different times in a day). For a given satellite pixel size, the temporal variability within the same satellite pixels increases with the sampling time difference over the SMA. For a given small (e.g., ≤4 h) sampling time difference within the same satellite pixels, the temporal variability in the retrieved NO2 VC increases with the increasing spatial resolution over the SMA, Busan region, and the Los Angeles Basin. The results of this study have implications for future satellite design and\nretrieval interpretation and validation when comparing pixel data with\nlocal observations. In addition, the analyses presented in this study are\nequally applicable in model evaluation when comparing model grid values to\nlocal observations. Results from the Weather Research and Forecasting model\ncoupled with Chemistry (WRF-Chem) model indicate that the normalized\nsatellite SGV of tropospheric NO2 VC calculated in this study could\nserve as an upper bound to the satellite SGV of other species (e.g., CO and SO2) that share common source(s) with NO2 but have relatively longer lifetime.\n",
