  {
    "title": "Retrievals of tropospheric ozone profiles from the synergism of AIRS and OMI: methodology and validation",
    "authors": [
      "D. Fu",
      "S. Kulawik",
      "K. Miyazaki",
      "K. Bowman",
      "J. Worden",
      "A. Eldering",
      "N. Livesey",
      "J. Teixeira",
      "F. Irion",
      "R. Herman",
      "G. Osterman",
      "Xiong Liu",
      "P. Levelt",
      "A. Thompson",
      "M. Luo"
    ],
    "year": 2018,
    "doi": "10.5194/AMT-11-5587-2018",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Measurement Techniques",
    "citation_count": 71,
    "paper_id": "df8d7f00435506b3fa672660e6338fcc75173c17",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/df8d7f00435506b3fa672660e6338fcc75173c17",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The Tropospheric Emission Spectrometer (TES) on the A-Train Aura satellite\nwas designed to profile tropospheric ozone and its precursors, taking\nmeasurements from 2004 to 2018. Starting in 2008, TES global sampling of\ntropospheric ozone was gradually reduced in latitude, with global coverage\nstopping in 2011. To extend the record of TES, this work presents a\nmultispectral approach that will provide O3 data products with\nvertical resolution and measurement error similar to TES by combining the\nsingle-footprint thermal infrared (TIR) hyperspectral radiances from the Aqua\nAtmospheric Infrared Sounder (AIRS) instrument and the ultraviolet (UV)\nchannels from the Aura Ozone Monitoring Instrument (OMI). The joint\nAIRS+OMI O3 retrievals are processed through the MUlti-SpEctra,\nMUlti-SpEcies, MUlti-SEnsors (MUSES) retrieval algorithm. Comparisons of\ncollocated joint AIRS+OMI and TES to ozonesonde measurements show that both\nsystems have similar errors, with mean and standard deviation of the\ndifferences well within the estimated measurement error. AIRS+OMI and TES\nhave slightly different biases (within 5 parts per billion) vs. the sondes.\nBoth AIRS and OMI have wide swath widths (∼1650 km for AIRS; ∼2600 km for OMI) across satellite ground tracks. Consequently, the joint\nAIRS+OMI measurements have the potential to maintain TES vertical\nsensitivity while increasing coverage by 2 orders of magnitude, thus\nproviding an unprecedented new data set with which to quantify the evolution\nof tropospheric ozone.\n",
    "indexed": {
