  {
    "title": "Overview: Estimating and reporting uncertainties in remotely sensed atmospheric composition and temperature",
    "authors": [
      "T. von Clarmann",
      "D. Degenstein",
      "N. Livesey",
      "S. Bender",
      "A. Braverman",
      "A. Butz",
      "S. Compernolle",
      "R. Damadeo",
      "S. Dueck",
      "P. Eriksson",
      "B. Funke",
      "Margaret Johnson",
      "Y. Kasai",
      "A. Keppens",
      "A. Kleinert",
      "N. Kramarova",
      "A. Laeng",
      "B. Langerock",
      "V. Payne",
      "A. Rozanov",
      "Tomohiro O. Sato",
      "M. Schneider",
      "P. Sheese",
      "V. Sofieva",
      "G. Stiller",
      "Christian von Savigny",
      "D. Zawada"
    ],
    "year": 2020,
    "doi": "10.5194/AMT-13-4393-2020",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Measurement Techniques",
    "citation_count": 54,
    "paper_id": "2e3c53d8369cfba87eec0f1a80a028a9c79bed37",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2e3c53d8369cfba87eec0f1a80a028a9c79bed37",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Remote sensing of atmospheric state variables typically relies on the inverse\nsolution of the radiative transfer equation. An adequately characterized\nretrieval provides information on the uncertainties of the estimated state\nvariables as well as on how any constraint or a priori assumption affects\nthe estimate. Reported characterization data should be intercomparable between\ndifferent instruments, empirically validatable, grid-independent, usable without\ndetailed knowledge of the instrument or retrieval technique, traceable and still have reasonable data volume. The latter may force one to work with\nrepresentative rather than individual characterization data. Many errors derive\nfrom approximations and simplifications used in real-world retrieval schemes,\nwhich are reviewed in this paper, along with related error estimation schemes.\nThe main sources of uncertainty are measurement noise, calibration errors,\nsimplifications and idealizations in the radiative transfer model and retrieval\nscheme, auxiliary data errors, and uncertainties in atmospheric or instrumental parameters. Some of these errors affect the result in a random way, while\nothers chiefly cause a bias or are of mixed character. Beyond this, it is\nof utmost importance to know the influence of any constraint and prior\ninformation on the solution. While different instruments or retrieval schemes\nmay require different error estimation schemes, we provide a list of\nrecommendations which should help to unify retrieval error reporting.",
