    "title": "Evaluation of single-footprint AIRS CH4 profile retrieval uncertainties using aircraft profile measurements",
    "authors": [
      "S. Kulawik",
      "J. Worden",
      "Vivienne H. Payne",
      "Dejian Fu",
      "S. Wofsy",
      "K. McKain",
      "Colm Sweeney",
      "Bruce C. Daube Jr.",
      "Alan Lipton",
      "Igor Polonsky",
      "Yuguang He",
      "K. Cady-Pereira",
      "E. Dlugokencky",
      "Daniel J. Jacob",
      "Yi Yin"
    ],
    "year": 2020,
    "doi": "10.5194/amt-2020-145",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Measurement Techniques",
    "citation_count": 2,
    "paper_id": "0a9fcfd4da031fbf2b87c256ce22969d304f53b6",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a9fcfd4da031fbf2b87c256ce22969d304f53b6",
    "abstract": "Abstract. We evaluate the uncertainties of methane optimal estimation retrievals from single-footprint thermal infrared observations from the Atmospheric Infrared Sounder (AIRS). These retrievals are primarily sensitive to atmospheric methane in the mid-troposphere through the lower stratosphere\n(∼2 to ∼17 km). We compare them to in situ observations made from aircraft during the\nHIAPER Pole to Pole Observations (HIPPO) and Atmospheric Tomography Mission (ATom) campaigns, and\nfrom the NOAA GML aircraft network, between the surface and 5–13 km, across a range of\nyears, latitudes between 60∘ S to 80∘ N, and over land and ocean. After a\nglobal, pressure-dependent bias correction, we find that the land and ocean have similar biases\nand that the reported observation error (combined measurement and interference errors) of\n∼27 ppb is consistent with the SD between aircraft and individual AIRS\nobservations. A single observation has measurement (noise related) uncertainty of\n∼17 ppb, a ∼20 ppb uncertainty from radiative interferences (e.g., from\nwater or temperature), and ∼30 ppb due to “smoothing error”, which is partially\nremoved when making comparisons to in situ measurements or models in a way that accounts for this\nregularization. We estimate a 10 ppb validation uncertainty because the aircraft typically\ndid not measure methane at altitudes where the AIRS measurements have some sensitivity, e.g., the\nstratosphere, and there is uncertainty in the truth that we validate against. Daily averaging only\npartly reduces the difference between aircraft and satellite observation, likely because of\ncorrelated errors introduced into the retrieval from temperature and water vapor. For example,\naveraging nine observations only reduces the aircraft–model difference to ∼17 ppb\nvs. the expected ∼10 ppb. Seasonal averages can reduce this ∼17 ppb\nuncertainty further to ∼10 ppb, as determined through comparison with NOAA aircraft,\nlikely because uncertainties related to radiative effects of temperature and water vapor are\nreduced when averaged over a season.\n",
