  {
    "title": "Characterization and evaluation of AIRS-based estimates of the deuterium content of water vapor",
    "authors": [
      "J. Worden",
      "S. Kulawik",
      "D. Fu",
      "V. Payne",
      "A. Lipton",
      "I. Polonsky",
      "Yuguang He",
      "K. Cady-Pereira",
      "J. Moncet",
      "R. Herman",
      "F. Irion",
      "K. Bowman"
    ],
    "year": 2018,
    "doi": "10.5194/AMT-12-2331-2019",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Measurement Techniques",
    "citation_count": 37,
    "paper_id": "519b625ec0022379130f6e971c77522993f7737e",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/519b625ec0022379130f6e971c77522993f7737e",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Single-pixel tropospheric retrievals of HDO and H2O concentrations\nare retrieved from Atmospheric Infrared Sounder (AIRS) radiances using the\noptimal estimation algorithm developed for the Aura Tropospheric Emission\nSpectrometer (TES) project. We evaluate the error characteristics and\nvertical sensitivity of AIRS measurements corresponding to 5 d of TES data\n(or five global surveys) during the Northern Hemisphere summers between 2006\nand 2010 (∼600 co-located comparisons per day). We find that the\nretrieval characteristics of the AIRS deuterium content measurements have\nsimilar vertical resolution in the middle troposphere as TES but with\nslightly less sensitivity in the lowermost troposphere, with a typical\ndegrees of freedom (DOFS) in the tropics of 1.5. The calculated measurement\nuncertainty is ∼30 ‰ (parts per\nthousand relative to the deuterium composition of ocean water) for a\ntropospheric average between 750 and 350 hPa, the altitude region where AIRS\nis most sensitive, compared to ∼15 ‰ for the TES data.\nComparison with the TES data also indicates that the uncertainty of a single\ntarget AIRS HDO ∕ H2O measurement is ∼30 ‰.\nComparison of AIRS and TES data between 30∘ S and 50∘ N\nindicates that the AIRS data are biased low by ∼-2.6 ‰ with a\nlatitudinal variation of ∼7.8 ‰. This latitudinal variation is\nconsistent with the accuracy of TES data compared to in situ measurements,\nsuggesting that both AIRS and TES have similar accuracy.\n",
    "indexed": {
