  {
    "title": "Hyperfine-resolution mapping of on-road vehicle emissions with comprehensive traffic monitoring and an intelligent transportation system",
    "authors": [
      "Linhui Jiang",
      "Y. Xia",
      "Lu Wang",
      "Xue Chen",
      "Jianjie Ye",
      "T. Hou",
      "Liqiang Wang",
      "Yibo Zhang",
      "Mengying Li",
      "Zhen Li",
      "Zhe Song",
      "Yaping Jiang",
      "Weiping Liu",
      "Pengfei Li",
      "D. Rosenfeld",
      "J. Seinfeld",
      "Shaocai Yu"
    ],
    "year": 2021,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-21-16985-2021",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 10,
    "paper_id": "6243a6a1bb65fddb45254b4ffe57d52a14164fce",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6243a6a1bb65fddb45254b4ffe57d52a14164fce",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Urban on-road vehicle emissions affect air quality and human health locally\nand globally. Given uneven sources, they typically exhibit distinct spatial\nheterogeneity, varying sharply over short distances (10 m–1 km). However, all-around observational constraints on the emission sources\nare limited in much of the world. Consequently, traditional emission\ninventories lack the spatial resolution that can characterize the on-road\nvehicle emission hotspots. Here we establish a bottom-up approach to reveal\na unique pattern of urban on-road vehicle emissions at a spatial resolution 1–3 orders of magnitude higher than current emission\ninventories. We interconnect all-around traffic monitoring (including\ntraffic fluxes, vehicle-specific categories, and speeds) via an intelligent\ntransportation system (ITS) over Xiaoshan District in the Yangtze River\nDelta (YRD) region. This enables us to calculate single-vehicle-specific\nemissions over each fine-scale (10 m–1 km) road segment. Thus,\nthe most hyperfine emission dataset of its type is achieved, and on-road\nemission hotspots appear. The resulting map shows that the hourly average\non-road vehicle emissions of CO, NOx, HC, and PM2.5 are 74.01,\n40.35, 8.13, and 1.68 kg, respectively. More importantly, widespread and\npersistent emission hotspots emerged. They are of significantly sharp\nsmall-scale variability, up to 8–15 times within individual\nhotspots, attributable to distinct traffic fluxes, road conditions, and\nvehicle categories. On this basis, we investigate the effectiveness of\nroutine traffic control strategies on on-road vehicle emission mitigation.\nOur results have important implications for how the strategies should be\ndesigned and optimized. Integrating our traffic-monitoring-based approach\nwith urban air quality measurements, we could address major data gaps\nbetween urban air pollutant emissions and concentrations.\n",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2021,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
