  {
    "title": "Validation of tropospheric NO2 column measurements of GOME-2A and OMI using MAX-DOAS and direct sun network observations",
    "authors": [
      "G. Pinardi",
      "M. Van Roozendael",
      "F. Hendrick",
      "N. Theys",
      "N. Abuhassan",
      "A. Bais",
      "F. Boersma",
      "A. Cede",
      "J. Chong",
      "Sebastian Donner",
      "T. Drosoglou",
      "Anatoly Dzhola",
      "H. Eskes",
      "U. Friess",
      "J. Granville",
      "J. Herman",
      "R. Holla",
      "J. Hovila",
      "Hitoshi Irie",
      "Y. Kanaya",
      "Dimitris Karagkiozidis",
      "N. Kouremeti",
      "J.-C. Lambert",
      "Jianzhong Ma",
      "E. Peters",
      "A. Piters",
      "O. Postylyakov",
      "Andreas Richter",
      "Julia Remmers",
      "H. Takashima",
      "Martin Tiefengraber",
      "P. Valks",
      "T. Vlemmix",
      "Thomas Wagner",
      "F. Wittrock"
    ],
    "year": 2020,
    "doi": "10.5194/amt-2020-76",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Measurement Techniques",
    "citation_count": 38,
    "paper_id": "c26ca8292ed60c3acb320473c704a07ab9d7cb0b",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c26ca8292ed60c3acb320473c704a07ab9d7cb0b",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Multi-axis differential optical absorption spectroscopy (MAX-DOAS) and direct sun NO2\nvertical column network data are used to investigate the accuracy of tropospheric NO2\ncolumn measurements of the GOME-2 instrument on the MetOp-A satellite platform and the OMI\ninstrument on Aura. The study is based on 23 MAX-DOAS and 16 direct sun instruments at stations\ndistributed worldwide. A method to quantify and correct for horizontal dilution effects in\nheterogeneous NO2 field conditions is proposed. After systematic application of this\ncorrection to urban sites, satellite measurements are found to present smaller biases compared to\nground-based reference data in almost all cases. We investigate the seasonal dependence of the\nvalidation results as well as the impact of using different approaches to select satellite ground\npixels in coincidence with ground-based data. In optimal comparison conditions (satellite pixels\ncontaining the station) the median bias between satellite tropospheric NO2 column\nmeasurements and the ensemble of MAX-DOAS and direct sun measurements is found to be significant\nand equal to −34 % for GOME-2A and −24 % for OMI. These biases are further reduced to\n−24 % and −18 % respectively, after application of the dilution\ncorrection. Comparisons with the QA4ECV satellite product for both GOME-2A and OMI are also\nperformed, showing less scatter but also a slightly larger median tropospheric NO2\ncolumn bias with respect to the ensemble of MAX-DOAS and direct sun measurements.\n",
    "indexed": {
