  {
    "title": "Effect of changing NOx lifetime on the seasonality and long-term trends of satellite-observed tropospheric NO2 columns over China",
    "authors": [
      "Viral Shah",
      "D. Jacob",
      "Ke Li",
      "R. Silvern",
      "S. Zhai",
      "Mengyao Liu",
      "Jintai Lin",
      "Qiang Zhang",
      "J. Paulson"
    ],
    "year": 2019,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-20-1483-2020",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 193,
    "paper_id": "bdaa991205d6eb2103f493fc4764314af7ae11e2",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bdaa991205d6eb2103f493fc4764314af7ae11e2",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Satellite observations of tropospheric NO2 columns\nare extensively used to infer trends in anthropogenic emissions of nitrogen\noxides (NOx≡NO+NO2), but this may be complicated by\ntrends in NOx lifetime. Here we use 2004–2018 observations from the\nOzone Monitoring Instrument (OMI) satellite-based instrument (QA4ECV and POMINO v2 retrievals) to examine\nthe seasonality and trends of tropospheric NO2 columns over\ncentral–eastern China, and we interpret the results with the GEOS-Chem\nchemical transport model. The observations show a factor of 3 increase in\nNO2 columns from summer to winter, which we explain in GEOS-Chem as\nreflecting a longer NOx lifetime in winter than in summer (21 h versus 5.9 h in 2017). The 2005–2018 summer trends of OMI NO2 closely follow the trends in the Multi-resolution Emission Inventory for China (MEIC), with a rise over the 2005–2011 period and a 25 % decrease since. We find in GEOS-Chem no significant trend of the NOx lifetime in summer, supporting the emission trend reported by the MEIC. The winter trend of OMI\nNO2 is steeper than in summer over the entire period, which we\nattribute to a decrease in NOx lifetime at lower NOx emissions. Half of the NOx sink in winter is from N2O5 hydrolysis, which counterintuitively becomes more efficient as NOx emissions decrease due\nto less titration of ozone at night. The formation of organic nitrates also\nbecomes an increasing sink of NOx as NOx emissions decrease but emissions of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) do not.\n",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2019,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
