  {
    "title": "The unintended consequence of SO2 and NO2 regulations over China: increase of ammonia levels and impact on PM2.5 concentrations",
    "authors": [
      "Mathieu Lachâtre",
      "A. Fortems‐Cheiney",
      "G. Foret",
      "G. Siour",
      "G. Dufour",
      "L. Clarisse",
      "C. Clerbaux",
      "P. Coheur",
      "M. Van Damme",
      "M. Beekmann"
    ],
    "year": 2018,
    "doi": "10.5194/ACP-19-6701-2019",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 71,
    "paper_id": "c3a496d3d0144d8244029764598cd685fe7c967e",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c3a496d3d0144d8244029764598cd685fe7c967e",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Air pollution reaching hazardous levels in many Chinese cities\nhas been a major concern in China over the past decades. New policies have\nbeen applied to regulate anthropogenic pollutant emissions, leading to\nchanges in atmospheric composition and in particulate matter (PM)\nproduction. Increasing levels of atmospheric ammonia columns have been\nobserved by satellite during recent years. In particular, observations from\nthe Infrared Atmospheric Sounding Interferometer (IASI) reveal an increase of\nthese columns by 15 % and 65 % from 2011 to 2013 and 2015, respectively,\nover eastern China. In this paper we performed model simulations for 2011,\n2013 and 2015 in order to understand the origin of this increase and to\nquantify the link between ammonia and the inorganic components of particles:\nNH4(p)+/SO4(p)2-/NO3(p)-. Interannual change of\nmeteorology can be excluded as a reason: year 2015 meteorology leads to\nenhanced sulfate production over eastern China, which increases the ammonium\nand decreases the ammonia content, which is contrary to satellite\nobservations. Reductions in SO2 and NOx emissions\nfrom 2011 to 2015 of 37.5 % and 21 % respectively, as constrained from\nsatellite data, lead to decreased inorganic matter (by 14 % for\nNH4(p)++SO4(p)2-+NO3(p)-). This in turn leads to\nincreased gaseous NH3(g) tropospheric columns by as much as 24 %\nand 49 % (sampled corresponding to IASI data availability) from 2011 to\n2013 and 2015 respectively and thus can explain most of the observed\nincrease.\n",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2018,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
    "citing_team_paper": "Intercomparison of NOx emission inventories over East Asia",
