  {
    "title": "In situ ozone production is highly sensitive to volatile organic compounds in Delhi, India",
    "authors": [
      "Beth S. Nelson",
      "G. Stewart",
      "Will S. Drysdale",
      "M. Newland",
      "A. Vaughan",
      "R. Dunmore",
      "P. Edwards",
      "A. Lewis",
      "J. Hamilton",
      "W. Acton",
      "C. Hewitt",
      "L. Crilley",
      "M. S. Alam",
      "Ü. Şahin",
      "D. Beddows",
      "W. Bloss",
      "Eloise J. Slater",
      "L. Whalley",
      "D. Heard",
      "J. Cash",
      "B. Langford",
      "E. Nemitz",
      "R. Sommariva",
      "S. Cox",
      "R. Gadi",
      "B. Gurjar",
      "J. Hopkins",
      "A. Rickard",
      "James D. Lee"
    ],
    "year": 2021,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-21-13609-2021",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 55,
    "paper_id": "d8baf784027b3d3da748863f69ccd767460929f8",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d8baf784027b3d3da748863f69ccd767460929f8",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The Indian megacity of Delhi suffers from some of the\npoorest air quality in the world. While ambient NO2 and particulate\nmatter (PM) concentrations have received considerable attention in the city,\nhigh ground-level ozone (O3) concentrations are an often overlooked\ncomponent of pollution. O3 can lead to significant ecosystem damage and\nagricultural crop losses, and adversely affect human health. During October 2018,\nconcentrations of speciated non-methane hydrocarbon volatile organic\ncompounds (C2–C13), oxygenated volatile organic compounds\n(o-VOCs), NO, NO2, HONO, CO, SO2, O3, and photolysis rates,\nwere continuously measured at an urban site in Old Delhi. These observations\nwere used to constrain a detailed chemical box model utilising the Master\nChemical Mechanism v3.3.1. VOCs and NOx (NO + NO2) were varied\nin the model to test their impact on local O3 production rates,\nP(O3), which revealed a VOC-limited chemical regime. When only NOx\nconcentrations were reduced, a significant increase in P(O3) was\nobserved; thus, VOC co-reduction approaches must also be considered in\npollution abatement strategies. Of the VOCs examined in this work, mean\nmorning P(O3) rates were most sensitive to monoaromatic compounds,\nfollowed by monoterpenes and alkenes, where halving their concentrations in\nthe model led to a 15.6 %, 13.1 %, and 12.9 % reduction in\nP(O3), respectively. P(O3) was not sensitive to direct changes in\naerosol surface area but was very sensitive to changes in photolysis rates,\nwhich may be influenced by future changes in PM concentrations. VOC and\nNOx concentrations were divided into emission source sectors, as\ndescribed by the Emissions Database for Global Atmospheric Research (EDGAR) v5.0 Global Air Pollutant Emissions and EDGAR\nv4.3.2_VOC_spec inventories, allowing for the\nimpact of individual emission sources on P(O3) to be investigated.\nReducing road transport emissions only, a common strategy in air pollution\nabatement strategies worldwide, was found to increase P(O3), even when\nthe source was removed in its entirety. Effective reduction in P(O3)\nwas achieved by reducing road transport along with emissions from combustion\nfor manufacturing and process emissions. Modelled P(O3) reduced by\n∼ 20 ppb h−1 when these combined sources were halved. This study\nhighlights the importance of reducing VOCs in parallel with NOx and PM\nin future pollution abatement strategies in Delhi.\n",
    "indexed": {
