  {
    "title": "Low-NO atmospheric oxidation pathways in a polluted megacity",
    "authors": [
      "M. Newland",
      "D. Bryant",
      "R. Dunmore",
      "T. Bannan",
      "W. Acton",
      "B. Langford",
      "J. Hopkins",
      "F. Squires",
      "William J. Dixon",
      "Will S. Drysdale",
      "P. Ivatt",
      "M. Evans",
      "Peter M. Edwards",
      "L. Whalley",
      "D. Heard",
      "Eloise J. Slater",
      "Robert Woodward-Massey",
      "C. Ye",
      "Archit Mehra",
      "S. Worrall",
      "A. Bacak",
      "H. Coe",
      "C. Percival",
      "C. Hewitt",
      "James D. Lee",
      "T. Cui",
      "J. Surratt",
      "Xinming Wang",
      "A. Lewis",
      "A. Rickard",
      "J. Hamilton"
    ],
    "year": 2021,
    "doi": "10.5194/ACP-21-1613-2021",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 39,
    "paper_id": "71b63b61834f6055987d7ad6b59ec5efb39dae0c",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/71b63b61834f6055987d7ad6b59ec5efb39dae0c",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The impact of emissions of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) to the\natmosphere on the production of secondary pollutants, such as ozone and\nsecondary organic aerosol (SOA), is mediated by the concentration of nitric\noxide (NO). Polluted urban atmospheres are typically considered to be\n“high-NO” environments, while remote regions such as rainforests, with\nminimal anthropogenic influences, are considered to be “low NO”. However,\nour observations from central Beijing show that this simplistic separation\nof regimes is flawed. Despite being in one of the largest megacities in the\nworld, we observe formation of gas- and aerosol-phase oxidation products\nusually associated with low-NO “rainforest-like” atmospheric oxidation\npathways during the afternoon, caused by extreme suppression of NO\nconcentrations at this time. Box model calculations suggest that during\nthe morning high-NO chemistry predominates (95 %) but in the afternoon\nlow-NO chemistry plays a greater role (30 %). Current emissions\ninventories are applied in the GEOS-Chem model which shows that such models,\nwhen run at the regional scale, fail to accurately predict such an extreme\ndiurnal cycle in the NO concentration. With increasing global emphasis on\nreducing air pollution, it is crucial for the modelling tools used to\ndevelop urban air quality policy to be able to accurately represent such\nextreme diurnal variations in NO to accurately predict the formation of\npollutants such as SOA and ozone.\n",
    "indexed": {
