  {
    "title": "On the role of trend and variability in the hydroxyl radical (OH) in the global methane budget",
    "authors": [
      "Yuanhong Zhao",
      "M. Saunois",
      "P. Bousquet",
      "Xin Lin",
      "A. Berchet",
      "M. Hegglin",
      "J. Canadell",
      "R. Jackson",
      "M. Deushi",
      "P. Jöckel",
      "D. Kinnison",
      "O. Kirner",
      "S. Strode",
      "S. Tilmes",
      "E. Dlugokencky",
      "B. Zheng"
    ],
    "year": 2020,
    "doi": "10.5194/ACP-20-13011-2020",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 51,
    "paper_id": "7289a712eaa813be6cb8fb2bb9b5b8fbb84fc38e",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7289a712eaa813be6cb8fb2bb9b5b8fbb84fc38e",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Decadal trends and interannual variations in the hydroxyl radical (OH),\nwhile poorly constrained at present, are critical for understanding the\nobserved evolution of atmospheric methane (CH4). Through analyzing the\nOH fields simulated by the model ensemble of the Chemistry–Climate Model\nInitiative (CCMI), we find (1) the negative OH anomalies during the El\nNiño years mainly corresponding to the enhanced carbon monoxide (CO)\nemissions from biomass burning and (2) a positive OH trend during 1980–2010\ndominated by the elevated primary production and the reduced loss of OH due\nto decreasing CO after 2000. Both two-box model inversions and variational\n4D inversions suggest that ignoring the negative anomaly of OH during the El Niño years leads to a large overestimation of the increase in global\nCH4 emissions by up to 10 ± 3 Tg yr−1 to match the observed\nCH4 increase over these years. Not accounting for the increasing OH\ntrends given by the CCMI models leads to an underestimation of the CH4\nemission increase by 23 ± 9 Tg yr−1 from 1986 to 2010. The\nvariational-inversion-estimated CH4 emissions show that the tropical\nregions contribute most to the uncertainties related to OH. This study\nhighlights the significant impact of climate and chemical feedbacks related\nto OH on the top-down estimates of the global CH4 budget.\n",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2020,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
