  {
    "title": "Net ozone production and its relationship to nitrogen oxides and volatile organic compounds in the marine boundary layer around the Arabian Peninsula",
    "authors": [
      "Ivan Tadic",
      "J. Crowley",
      "D. Dienhart",
      "Philipp G. Eger",
      "H. Harder",
      "B. Hottmann",
      "Miguel Ángel Martínez",
      "U. Parchatka",
      "J. Paris",
      "A. Pozzer",
      "Roland Rohloff",
      "J. Schuladen",
      "J. Shenolikar",
      "S. Tauer",
      "J. Lelieveld",
      "H. Fischer"
    ],
    "year": 2020,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-20-6769-2020",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 53,
    "paper_id": "4c7a7408b387a27a76b85b7afc4b5f6c89fcd946",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4c7a7408b387a27a76b85b7afc4b5f6c89fcd946",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Strongly enhanced tropospheric ozone (O3) mixing ratios have\nbeen reported in the Arabian Basin, a region with intense solar radiation\nand high concentrations of O3 precursors such as nitrogen oxides (NOx) and\nvolatile organic compounds (VOCs). To analyze photochemical O3 production in the\nmarine boundary layer (MBL) around the Arabian Peninsula, we use shipborne\nobservations of NO, NO2, O3, OH, HO2, HCHO, the actinic flux,\nwater vapor, pressure and temperature obtained during the summer 2017 Air\nQuality and Climate in the Arabian Basin (AQABA) campaign, and we compare them to\nsimulation results from the ECHAM-MESSy Atmospheric Chemistry (EMAC) general\ncirculation model. Net O3 production rates (NOPRs) were greatest over both the Gulf of Oman and the northern Red Sea (16 ppbv d−1) and\nover the Arabian Gulf (32 ppbv d−1). The NOPR over the\nMediterranean, the southern Red Sea and the Arabian Sea did not\nsignificantly deviate from zero; however, the results for the Arabian Sea\nindicated weak net O3 production of 5 ppbv d−1 as well as net O3\ndestruction over the Mediterranean and the southern Red Sea with values of −1 and −4 ppbv d−1, respectively. Constrained\nby HCHO∕NO2 ratios, our photochemistry calculations show that net O3\nproduction in the MBL around the Arabian Peninsula mostly occurs in\nNOx-limited regimes with a significant share of O3 production\noccurring in the transition regime between NOx limitation and VOC limitation over\nthe Mediterranean and more significantly over the northern Red Sea and Oman\nGulf.\n",
    "indexed": {
