  {
    "title": "Trends in global tropospheric hydroxyl radical and methane lifetime since 1850 from AerChemMIP",
    "authors": [
      "D. Stevenson",
      "Alcide Zhao",
      "V. Naik",
      "Fiona M. O'Connor",
      "S. Tilmes",
      "G. Zeng",
      "L. Murray",
      "William J. Collins",
      "P. T. Griffiths",
      "S. Shim",
      "L. Horowitz",
      "L. T. Sentman",
      "L. Emmons"
    ],
    "year": 2020,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-2019-1219",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 73,
    "paper_id": "44c9789e771f015f86c9ac75fa297332d557e459",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/44c9789e771f015f86c9ac75fa297332d557e459",
    "abstract": "Abstract. We analyse historical (1850–2014) atmospheric hydroxyl\n(OH) and methane lifetime data from Coupled Model Intercomparison Project\nPhase 6 (CMIP6)/Aerosols and Chemistry Model Intercomparison Project\n(AerChemMIP) simulations. Tropospheric OH changed little from 1850 up to\naround 1980, then increased by around 9 % up to 2014, with an associated\nreduction in methane lifetime. The model-derived OH trends from 1980 to 2005\nare broadly consistent with trends estimated by several studies that infer\nOH from inversions of methyl chloroform and associated measurements; most\ninversion studies indicate decreases in OH since 2005. However, the model\nresults fall within observational uncertainty ranges. The upward trend in\nmodelled OH since 1980 was mainly driven by changes in anthropogenic\nnear-term climate forcer emissions (increases in anthropogenic nitrogen\noxides and decreases in CO). Increases in halocarbon emissions since 1950\nhave made a small contribution to the increase in OH, whilst increases in\naerosol-related emissions have slightly reduced OH. Halocarbon emissions\nhave dramatically reduced the stratospheric methane lifetime by about\n15 %–40 %; most previous studies assumed a fixed stratospheric lifetime.\nWhilst the main driver of atmospheric methane increases since 1850 is\nemissions of methane itself, increased ozone precursor emissions have\nsignificantly modulated (in general reduced) methane trends. Halocarbon and\naerosol emissions are found to have relatively small contributions to\nmethane trends. These experiments do not isolate the effects of climate\nchange on OH and methane evolution; however, we calculate residual terms that\nare due to the combined effects of climate change and non-linear\ninteractions between drivers. These residual terms indicate that non-linear\ninteractions are important and differ between the two methodologies we use\nfor quantifying OH and methane drivers. All these factors need to be\nconsidered in order to fully explain OH and methane trends since 1850; these\nfactors will also be important for future trends.\n",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2020,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
