  {
    "title": "Evaluation of version 3.0B of the BEHR OMI NO2 product",
    "authors": [
      "J. Laughner",
      "Qindan Zhu",
      "R. Cohen"
    ],
    "year": 2019,
    "doi": "10.5194/AMT-12-129-2019",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Measurement Techniques",
    "citation_count": 36,
    "paper_id": "630beff93baa7875b6c8e9731938e3b00f529b04",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/630beff93baa7875b6c8e9731938e3b00f529b04",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Version 3.0B of the Berkeley High Resolution (BEHR) Ozone Monitoring Instrument (OMI) NO2 product is\ndesigned to accurately retrieve daily variation in the\nhigh-spatial-resolution mapping of tropospheric column NO2 over\ncontinental North America between 25 and 50∘ N. To assess the\nproduct, we compare against in situ aircraft profiles and Pandora vertical\ncolumn densities (VCDs). We also compare the WRF-Chem simulation used to\ngenerate the a priori NO2 profiles against observations. We find that\nusing daily NO2 profiles improves the VCDs retrieved in urban areas\nrelative to low-resolution or monthly a priori NO2 profiles by\namounts that are large compared to current uncertainties in\nNOx emissions and chemistry (of the order of 10 % to\n30 %). Based on this analysis, we offer suggestions to consider when\ndesigning retrieval algorithms and validation procedures for upcoming\ngeostationary satellites.\n",
    "indexed": {
