  {
    "title": "The Environment and Climate Change Canada Carbon Assimilation System (EC-CAS v1.0): demonstration with simulated CO observations",
    "authors": [
      "V. Khade",
      "S. Polavarapu",
      "M. Neish",
      "P. Houtekamer",
      "Dylan B. A. Jones",
      "Seung-Jong Baek",
      "Tai-Long He",
      "S. Gravel"
    ],
    "year": 2020,
    "doi": "10.5194/gmd-2020-219",
    "venue": "Geoscientific Model Development",
    "citation_count": 1,
    "paper_id": "feb38745a21c9f5f4d929d9f61773432c489ff7d",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/feb38745a21c9f5f4d929d9f61773432c489ff7d",
    "abstract": "Abstract. In this study, we present the development of a new coupled weather and carbon monoxide (CO) data\nassimilation system based on the Environment and Climate Change Canada (ECCC) operational\nensemble Kalman filter (EnKF). The estimated meteorological state is augmented to include CO.\nVariable localization is used to\nprevent the direct update of meteorology by the observations of the constituents and\nvice versa. Physical localization\nis used to damp spurious analysis increments far from a given observation. Perturbed surface flux fields\nare used to account for the uncertainty in CO due to errors in the surface fluxes. The system is\ndemonstrated for the estimation of three-dimensional CO states using simulated observations\nfrom a variety of networks. First, a hypothetically dense, uniformly distributed observation\nnetwork is used to demonstrate that the system is working. More realistic observation networks,\nbased on surface hourly observations, and space-based observations provide a demonstration of\nthe complementarity of the different networks and further confirm the reasonable behavior\nof the coupled assimilation system. Having demonstrated the ability to estimate CO\ndistributions, this system will be extended to estimate surface fluxes in the future.\n",
    "indexed": {
