  {
    "title": "Attribution of the accelerating increase in atmospheric methane during 2010–2018 by inverse analysis of GOSAT observations",
    "authors": [
      "Yuzhong Zhang",
      "D. Jacob",
      "Xiao Lu",
      "J. Maasakkers",
      "T. Scarpelli",
      "J. Sheng",
      "Lu Shen",
      "Z. Qu",
      "M. Sulprizio",
      "Jinfeng Chang",
      "A. Bloom",
      "Shuang Ma",
      "J. Worden",
      "R. Parker",
      "H. Boesch"
    ],
    "year": 2020,
    "doi": "10.5194/ACP-21-3643-2021",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 124,
    "paper_id": "bd7bf59ed6031ebe034f7d97658aac77c5acf054",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bd7bf59ed6031ebe034f7d97658aac77c5acf054",
    "abstract": "Abstract. We conduct a global inverse analysis of 2010–2018 GOSAT\nobservations to better understand the factors controlling\natmospheric methane and its accelerating increase over the 2010–2018\nperiod. The inversion optimizes anthropogenic methane emissions and their\n2010–2018 trends on a 4∘×5∘\ngrid, monthly regional wetland emissions, and annual hemispheric\nconcentrations of tropospheric OH (the main sink of methane). We use an\nanalytical solution to the Bayesian optimization problem that provides\nclosed-form estimates of error covariances and information content for the\nsolution. We verify our inversion results with independent methane\nobservations from the TCCON and NOAA networks. Our inversion successfully\nreproduces the interannual variability of the methane growth rate inferred\nfrom NOAA background sites. We find that prior estimates of fuel-related\nemissions reported by individual countries to the United Nations are too\nhigh for China (coal) and Russia (oil and gas) and too low for Venezuela\n(oil and gas) and the US (oil and gas). We show large 2010–2018 increases in\nanthropogenic methane emissions over South Asia, tropical Africa, and\nBrazil, coincident with rapidly growing livestock populations in these\nregions. We do not find a significant trend in anthropogenic emissions over\nregions with high rates of production or use of fossil methane, including the US,\nRussia, and Europe. Our results indicate that the peak methane growth rates\nin 2014–2015 are driven by low OH concentrations (2014) and high fire\nemissions (2015), while strong emissions from tropical (Amazon and tropical\nAfrica) and boreal (Eurasia) wetlands combined with increasing anthropogenic\nemissions drive high growth rates in 2016–2018. Our best estimate is that\nOH did not contribute significantly to the 2010–2018 methane trend other\nthan the 2014 spike, though error correlation with global anthropogenic\nemissions limits confidence in this result.\n",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2020,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
