  {
    "title": "Inter-model comparison of global hydroxyl radical (OH) distributions and their impact on atmospheric methane over the 2000–2016 period",
    "authors": [
      "Yuanhong Zhao",
      "M. Saunois",
      "P. Bousquet",
      "Xin Lin",
      "A. Berchet",
      "M. Hegglin",
      "J. Canadell",
      "R. Jackson",
      "D. Hauglustaine",
      "S. Szopa",
      "A. Stavert",
      "N. Abraham",
      "A. Archibald",
      "S. Bekki",
      "M. Deushi",
      "P. Jöckel",
      "B. Josse",
      "D. Kinnison",
      "O. Kirner",
      "V. Marécal",
      "F. O’Connor",
      "D. Plummer",
      "L. Revell",
      "E. Rozanov",
      "A. Stenke",
      "S. Strode",
      "S. Tilmes",
      "E. Dlugokencky",
      "B. Zheng"
    ],
    "year": 2019,
    "doi": "10.5194/ACP-19-13701-2019",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 94,
    "paper_id": "9cfbc289e092c0086c7864ffe6eadfc33f96e048",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9cfbc289e092c0086c7864ffe6eadfc33f96e048",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The modeling study presented here aims to estimate how uncertainties in\nglobal hydroxyl radical (OH) distributions, variability, and trends may\ncontribute to resolving discrepancies between simulated and observed methane\n(CH4) changes since 2000. A multi-model ensemble of 14 OH fields was\nanalyzed and aggregated into 64 scenarios to force the offline\natmospheric chemistry transport model LMDz (Laboratoire de Meteorologie Dynamique) with a standard CH4 emission\nscenario over the period 2000–2016. The multi-model simulated global\nvolume-weighted tropospheric mean OH concentration ([OH]) averaged over\n2000–2010 ranges between 8.7×105 and 12.8×105 molec cm−3. The inter-model differences in tropospheric OH burden and\nvertical distributions are mainly determined by the differences in the\nnitrogen oxide (NO) distributions, while the spatial discrepancies between\nOH fields are mostly due to differences in natural emissions and volatile organic compound (VOC)\nchemistry. From 2000 to 2010, most simulated OH fields show an increase of\n0.1–0.3×105 molec cm−3 in the tropospheric mean [OH],\nwith year-to-year variations much smaller than during the historical period\n1960–2000. Once ingested into the LMDz model, these OH changes translated\ninto a 5 to 15 ppbv reduction in the CH4 mixing ratio in 2010, which\nrepresents 7 %–20 % of the model-simulated CH4 increase due to\nsurface emissions. Between 2010 and 2016, the ensemble of simulations showed\nthat OH changes could lead to a CH4 mixing ratio uncertainty of\n>±30 ppbv. Over the full 2000–2016 time period, using a\ncommon state-of-the-art but nonoptimized emission scenario, the impact of\n[OH] changes tested here can explain up to 54 % of the gap between model\nsimulations and observations. This result emphasizes the importance of\nbetter representing OH abundance and variations in CH4 forward\nsimulations and emission optimizations performed by atmospheric inversions.\n",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2019,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
