  {
    "title": "History of chemically and radiatively important atmospheric gases from the Advanced Global Atmospheric Gases Experiment (AGAGE)",
    "authors": [
      "R. Prinn",
      "R. Weiss",
      "J. Arduini",
      "T. Arnold",
      "H. Langley DeWitt",
      "P. Fraser",
      "L. Ganesan",
      "J. Gasore",
      "C. Harth",
      "O. Hermansen",
      "Jooil Kim",
      "P. Krummel",
      "Shanlan Li",
      "Zoë M. Loh",
      "C. Lunder",
      "M. Maione",
      "A. Manning",
      "Ben R. Miller",
      "B. Mitrevski",
      "J. Mühle",
      "S. O'Doherty",
      "Sunyoung Park",
      "S. Reimann",
      "M. Rigby",
      "Takuya Saito",
      "P. Salameh",
      "R. Schmidt",
      "P. Simmonds",
      "L. Steele",
      "8. MartinK.",
      "Vollmer",
      "Ray H. J. Wang",
      "B. Yao",
      "Y. Yokouchi",
      "D. Young",
      "Ling-xi Zhou"
    ],
    "year": 2018,
    "doi": "10.5194/ESSD-10-985-2018",
    "venue": "Earth System Science Data",
    "citation_count": 259,
    "paper_id": "c0b038072693fed4cbd13cc837a5d078427562b0",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c0b038072693fed4cbd13cc837a5d078427562b0",
    "abstract": "Abstract. We present the organization, instrumentation, datasets, data interpretation,\nmodeling, and accomplishments of the multinational global atmospheric\nmeasurement program AGAGE (Advanced Global Atmospheric Gases Experiment).\nAGAGE is distinguished by its capability to measure globally, at high\nfrequency, and at multiple sites all the important species in the Montreal\nProtocol and all the important non-carbon-dioxide (non-CO2) gases\nassessed by the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (CO2 is\nalso measured at several sites). The scientific objectives of AGAGE are\nimportant in furthering our understanding of global chemical and climatic\nphenomena. They are the following: (1) to accurately measure the temporal and\nspatial distributions of anthropogenic gases that contribute the majority of\nreactive halogen to the stratosphere and/or are strong infrared absorbers\n(chlorocarbons, chlorofluorocarbons – CFCs, bromocarbons,\nhydrochlorofluorocarbons – HCFCs, hydrofluorocarbons – HFCs and\npolyfluorinated compounds (perfluorocarbons – PFCs), nitrogen trifluoride –\nNF3, sulfuryl fluoride – SO2F2, and sulfur hexafluoride –\nSF6) and use these measurements to determine the global rates of\ntheir emission and/or destruction (i.e., lifetimes); (2) to accurately\nmeasure the global distributions and temporal behaviors and determine the\nsources and sinks of non-CO2 biogenic–anthropogenic gases important\nto climate change and/or ozone depletion (methane – CH4, nitrous\noxide – N2O,\ncarbon monoxide – CO, molecular hydrogen – H2, methyl chloride\n– CH3Cl, and methyl bromide – CH3Br); (3) to identify new\nlong-lived greenhouse and ozone-depleting gases (e.g., SO2F2,\nNF3, heavy PFCs (C4F10, C5F12,\nC6F14, C7F16, and C8F18) and\nhydrofluoroolefins (HFOs; e.g., CH2 = CFCF3) have been\nidentified in AGAGE), initiate the real-time monitoring of these new gases,\nand reconstruct their past histories from AGAGE, air archive, and firn air\nmeasurements; (4) to determine the average concentrations and trends of\ntropospheric hydroxyl radicals (OH) from the rates of destruction of\natmospheric trichloroethane (CH3CCl3), HFCs, and HCFCs and estimates\nof their emissions; (5) to determine from atmospheric observations and\nestimates of their destruction rates the magnitudes and distributions by\nregion of surface sources and sinks of all measured gases; (6) to provide\naccurate data on the global accumulation of many of these trace gases that\nare used to test the synoptic-, regional-, and global-scale circulations\npredicted by three-dimensional models; and (7) to provide global and regional\nmeasurements of methane, carbon monoxide, and molecular hydrogen and\nestimates of hydroxyl levels to test primary atmospheric oxidation pathways\nat midlatitudes and the tropics. Network Information and Data Repository:\nhttp://agage.mit.edu/data or\nhttp://cdiac.ess-dive.lbl.gov/ndps/alegage.html\n(https://doi.org/10.3334/CDIAC/atg.db1001).\n",
    "indexed": {
