  {
    "title": "Simultaneous assimilation of ozone profiles from multiple UV-VIS satellite instruments",
    "authors": [
      "J. V. Peet",
      "A. Ronaldvander",
      "H. Kelder",
      "P. Levelt"
    ],
    "year": 2017,
    "doi": "10.5194/ACP-18-1685-2018",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 9,
    "paper_id": "ff8d7d8b12b0187139ab392fec2e2cdd1c068a78",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ff8d7d8b12b0187139ab392fec2e2cdd1c068a78",
    "abstract": "Abstract. A three-dimensional global ozone distribution has been derived from\nassimilation of ozone profiles that were observed by satellites. By\nsimultaneous assimilation of ozone profiles retrieved from the nadir\nlooking satellite instruments Global Ozone Monitoring Experiment 2\n(GOME-2) and Ozone Monitoring Instrument (OMI), which measure the\natmosphere at different times of the day, the quality of the derived\natmospheric ozone field has been improved. The assimilation is using\nan extended Kalman filter in which chemical transport model TM5 has\nbeen used for the forecast. The combined assimilation of both GOME-2\nand OMI improves upon the assimilation results of a single\nsensor. The new assimilation system has been demonstrated by\nprocessing 4 years of data from 2008 to 2011. Validation of the\nassimilation output by comparison with sondes shows that biases vary\nbetween − 5 and +10 % between the surface and\n100  hPa . The biases for the combined assimilation vary\nbetween − 3 and +3 % in the region between 100 and\n10  hPa where GOME-2 and OMI are most sensitive. This is\na strong improvement compared to direct retrievals of ozone profiles\nfrom satellite observations.",
    "indexed": {
      "date-parts": [
        [
          2017,
          1,
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
