  {
    "title": "Assimilation of S5P/TROPOMI carbon monoxide data with the global CAMS near-real-time system",
    "authors": [
      "A. Inness",
      "I. Aben",
      "M. Ades",
      "T. Borsdorff",
      "J. Flemming",
      "L. Jones",
      "J. Landgraf",
      "B. Langerock",
      "P. Nédélec",
      "M. Parrington",
      "Roberto Ribas"
    ],
    "year": 2022,
    "doi": "10.5194/acp-22-14355-2022",
    "venue": "Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics",
    "citation_count": 20,
    "paper_id": "0563a916ade80d30978933600e2640112dad845a",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0563a916ade80d30978933600e2640112dad845a",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The Tropospheric Monitoring Instrument (TROPOMI) on the Copernicus Sentinel 5 Precursor (S5P) satellite, launched in October 2017, provides a wealth of atmospheric composition data, including total columns of carbon monoxide (TCCO) at high horizontal resolution (5.5 km × 7 km).\nNear-real-time TROPOMI TCCO data have been monitored in the global data\nassimilation system of the Copernicus Atmosphere Monitoring Service (CAMS)\nsince November 2018 to assess the quality of the data. The CAMS system\nalready routinely assimilates TCCO data from the Measurement of Pollution in\nthe Troposphere (MOPITT) instrument and the Infrared Atmospheric Sounding\nInterferometer (IASI) outside the polar regions. The assimilation of TROPOMI TCCO data in the CAMS system was tested for the\nperiod 6 July to 31 December 2021, i.e. after the TROPOMI algorithm update to version 02.02.00 in July 2021. By assimilating TROPOMI TCCO observations, the CAMS CO columns increase by on average 8 %, resulting in an improved fit to independent observations (IAGOS aircraft profiles and NDACC Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) tropospheric and total-column CO data) compared to a version of the CAMS system where only TCCO from MOPITT and IASI is assimilated. The largest absolute and relative changes from the assimilation of TROPOMI CO are found in the lower and middle troposphere, i.e. that part of the atmosphere that is not already well constrained by the assimilated TIR MOPITT and IASI data. The largest impact near the surface comes from clear-sky TROPOMI data over land, and additional vertical information comes from the retrievals of measurements in cloudy conditions. July and August 2021 saw record numbers of boreal wildfires over North\nAmerica and Russia, leading to large amounts of CO being released into the\natmosphere. The paper assesses the impact of TROPOMI CO\nassimilation on selected CO plumes more closely. While the CO column can be well\nconstrained by the assimilation of TROPOMI CO data, and the fit to\nindividual IAGOS CO profiles in the lower and middle troposphere is considerably improved, the TROPOMI CO columns do not provide further\nconstraints on individual plumes that are transported across continents and\noceans at altitudes above 500 hPa.\n",
    "indexed": {
